  <h1>What Is Immunochromatography?</h1>
  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Immunochromatography is the antibody-based technique behind lateral-flow rapid tests — it uses antigen-antibody binding on a membrane to capture a target and produce a visible colored line, without any instrument.</p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>A sample is applied and migrates along the strip by capillary action.</li>
    <li>Labeled antibodies (e.g., colloidal gold) bind the target analyte.</li>
    <li>The complex is captured at the test line by immobilized antibodies, producing a visible line.</li>
    <li>The control line confirms the test ran correctly.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>Immunochromatography is the foundation of instrument-free rapid testing — it is why tests can be run anywhere, store at room temperature, and give results in minutes. "Lateral flow" and "immunochromatographic assay" describe the same technique.</p>

